WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 116 for July 28th, 2026.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.380
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.020
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:16.320
Let's dive in.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.460
Here's something that could change how you use your computer every single day.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.360
Proplexity has brought its personal computer tool to Windows.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.620
This means the world's most common operating system can now run an AI that acts like a general

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.420
purpose digital worker right on your own machine.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.940
Think of your computer like a messy desk covered in papers and folders.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.760
The AI steps in like a super organized assistant who can see everything on that desk, open

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.300
the right drawers, and put things in order without waiting for you to point at each item.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:54.300
It connects directly to your local files and apps so it can perform real actions instead

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.980
of just chatting back with answers.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:01.200
The Windows version works the same way an earlier Mac release did back in April.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.660
Your data stays private because everything runs on your own computer instead of sending

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.020
details somewhere else.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.760
This matters for students who spend hours sorting notes or pulling facts from different documents.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.600
The AI could gather those notes from scattered folders and turn them into one clean study

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.020
guide while you focus on other things.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:27.200
For someone exploring creative hobbies, it might pull reference images and text from your

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.220
saved files into a single place.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.920
You can download the tool from Proplexity's site and start testing it on everyday tasks

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.080
like organizing downloads or summarizing documents you already have.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.060
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.500
We are going to look under the hood at how AI agents actually work.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.780
Picture a friend helping you with a group project.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.360
That friend does not just suggest ideas and then stop.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:59.080
Instead the friend opens the shared folder, grabs the right files, and puts everything into

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.800
one document without needing a new instruction for each tiny step.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:04.780
Now add another layer.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:10.520
The same friend can also open a browser, search for missing information, copy it into the

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.580
right spot, and even send the finished file to the group.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.820
The friend keeps checking what still needs doing and moves to the next step on their own.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:22.000
An AI agent follows a similar pattern.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:27.760
It receives a goal, looks at the tools and files it can reach, then carries out a sequence

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.620
of actions to reach that goal.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.900
Instead of stopping after one answer, it keeps going through the steps.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.760
The way a person would click through apps or pages.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.060
The key difference is that the AI follows patterns it learned from seeing millions of similar

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:43.420
tasks.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.620
So it can guess the next useful move even when the exact situation is new.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.140
When the agent finishes, you see the result in your files or apps instead of just reading

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:55.580
a description of what happened.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.640
The process repeats as needed until the original request is complete using whatever resources

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.020
are available on the computer at that moment.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:09.620
That process is what turns a simple chat tool into something that actually gets work done

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:10.160
for you.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.720
Now let's look at two other tools you can try today.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.400
Knowing names of artistic styles and techniques can give you much more interesting results from

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.560
AI image tools.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:27.460
Words like vapor wave, bow house, sfumato, or art nouveau act as shortcuts that tell the

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.940
AI exactly what kind of look you want.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:35.880
Think of each style name like a single key on a piano that plays an entire chord instead

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:36.560
of one note.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.840
The AI already knows the whole mood and technique behind that word.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.940
So you do not have to describe every brush stroke or color choice.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:51.360
This is useful if you create art, design posters for school projects, or just like experimenting

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.760
with visuals for fun.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.880
Go to a free tool like Bing image creator or Grox image feature and type a simple prompt

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.760
such as a city street in vapor wave style.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:07.560
Notice how that single art term changes the entire result compared with a plain description.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.640
The same approach works with other terms such as mucarnas, art nouveau, whiplash curves,

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:17.960
grisyle, no tan, polysyndaton, and zugma.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.540
Each name carries a bundle of visual or structural rules that the AI already knows so one word

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.540
replaces paragraphs of explanation.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.060
Next up is something already inside an app millions of teens use every day.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:38.020
Meta has added its AI chatbot directly into the direct messages section of threads.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.720
You can have private conversations with the AI without leaving the app.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:48.700
Imagine switching between texting a friend and asking the AI for ideas all in the same chat list.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:54.280
If you have the threads app, open a new direct message and start a chat with Meta AI.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:05:00.120
Try asking it to help rewrite a caption for a post or brainstorm ideas for a school assignment.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:05.720
The conversations stay inside your existing direct message list so nothing extra needs

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.180
to be installed or opened.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.000
Two quick updates caught my eye this week.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.260
Baidu has begun testing itself driving cars in London with partners lift and free now.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.960
The cars are already running routes in the city showing how driverless technology is moving

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.940
from testing to real world use in more places.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:32.060
Open AI has brought live voice conversations in chat GPT to education, business, and enterprise

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:33.240
plans worldwide.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:39.360
This lets people talk naturally with the AI instead of only typing, making it easier for

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.560
students and teams to get help on the go.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.080
That's it for today.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:47.380
Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:51.400
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.180
That's literally how learning works.

